{"name":"Oregon Trail","hex":"#efb91b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Oregon Trail","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A large-wheeled wagon route and emigrant trail that traversed from east to west in the United States and connected the Missouri River to the valleys in Oregon Territory."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Oregon_Trail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oregon Trail","blocks":[{"text":"The Oregon Trail was a 2,170-mile (3,490 km) east–west, large-wheeled wagon route and emigrant trail in North America that connected the Missouri River to valleys in Oregon Territory. The eastern part of the Oregon Trail crossed what is now the states of Kansas, Nebraska, and Wyoming. The western half crossed the current states of Idaho and Oregon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Wpdms_nasa_topo_oregon_trail.jpg/330px-Wpdms_nasa_topo_oregon_trail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon_Trail","wikidata":"Q862312"},{"title":"Oregon","blocks":[{"text":"Oregon is a state in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States. It is a part of the Western United States, with the Columbia River delineating much of Oregon's northern boundary with Washington, while the Snake River delineates much of its eastern boundary with Idaho. The 42° north parallel delineates the southern boundary with California and Nevada. The western boundary is formed by the Pacific Ocean."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Flag_of_Oregon.svg/330px-Flag_of_Oregon.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oregon","wikidata":"Q824"},{"title":"Trail","blocks":[{"text":"A trail, also known as a path or track, is a dirt road or a small paved road, usually through an undeveloped natural area, that is designed primarily for use by pedestrians, equestrians and non-motorized vehicles, although occasionally some off-roading motor vehicles may also use it. In the United Kingdom and Ireland, footpath or hiking trail is the preferred term. However, it is sometimes applied to highways in North America. In the United States, the term was historically used to describe the westward expansion trails, which are land routes into or through wildness regions of the American West used by explorers and migrants."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg/330px-Trail_between_two_fields_%28Slovenia%2C_Selo_pri_Mirni%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trail","wikidata":"Q628179"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Oregontrail_1907.jpg/500px-Oregontrail_1907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Oregontrail_1907.jpg/1280px-Oregontrail_1907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Oregontrail_1907.jpg/500px-Oregontrail_1907.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOregontrail_1907.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Carte_Lewis-Clark_Expedition-en.png/500px-Carte_Lewis-Clark_Expedition-en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Carte_Lewis-Clark_Expedition-en.png/500px-Carte_Lewis-Clark_Expedition-en.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The route of the Lewis and Clark Expedition","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACarte_Lewis-Clark_Expedition-en.png"